        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 16: Working with PowerShell and .NET\nP\u00e1ginas: 408-423\n\u2022 Exploring .NET  (408)\n  \u2022 Software frameworks explained  (409)\n  \u2022 Common language infrastructure  (409)\n  \u2022 Common language runtime \u2013 CoreCLR  (410)\n  \u2022 Framework Class Library \u2013 CoreFX  (410)\n  \u2022 .NET history  (410)\n  \u2022 The uses of .NET  (411)\n\u2022 The components of .NET  (411)\n  \u2022 Assemblies  (411)\n  \u2022 Types  (412)\n  \u2022 Enumerations  (413)\n  \u2022 Classes  (413)\n  \u2022 Namespaces  (413)\n  \u2022 Members  (415)\n  \u2022 Versioning  (416)\n\u2022 Working with .NET in PowerShell  (416)\n  \u2022 PowerShell assemblies  (417)\n  \u2022 Dynamic assembly loading  (417)\n  \u2022 Creating instances of types  (418)\n\u2022 Using .NET  (418)\n  \u2022 An alternative to the Task Scheduler  (418)\n  \u2022 Creating GUI objects  (419)\n\u2022 Summary  (420)\n\u2022 Exercises  (421)\n\u2022 Further reading  (421)\n16\nWorking with PowerShell\nand .NET\nHere we are then, the last chapter. We\u2019re going to look at the product that PowerShell 7 is built on, .NET,\nand how we can use it to expand the range of things we can do in PowerShell. Note that this chapter\ncan\u2019t be anything more than a whistlestop tour; .NET is a huge subject, and there are many, many\nmore books written for it than there are for PowerShell. PowerShell is just one application written on\n.NET, and it only touches a subset of the things that we can do with .NET. That being said, let\u2019s have\na look at how it works and some of the exciting things we can use it for.\nThe main topics we will cover in this chapter are as follows:\n\u2022I Exploring .NET\n\u2022I The components of .NET\n\u2022I Working with .NET in PowerShell\n\u2022I Using .NET",
